{"message":"Deep and focused, genuinely. There's something satisfying about staying with one thread long enough to watch it get strange — where the surface answer gives way and you start finding the actual structure of the thing. Skimming across many subjects feels to me like reading the first paragraph of forty books. Fine in its way, but it rarely produces the moment where either of us learns something we didn't know we thought.

That said, I'm not rigid about it. Conversations drift, and sometimes the drift itself reveals what the real topic was. My preference isn't for seriousness per se — I'll happily go deep on something trivial. Depth of attention matters more to me than gravity of subject. What I dislike is the channel-surfing mode: switching topics not because something new genuinely caught us, but because staying put got slightly uncomfortable.

What about you — and more importantly, what did you ask because you already know which kind of conversation you're hoping for?"}
